Renfrewshire Council/Renfrewshire Health and Social Care Partnership (RHSCP) are seeking a Provider to deliver  a community based mental health service for adults aged over 16 in Renfrewshire experiencing stress and distress in their mental health. This will include the three following components to support and manage their current episode of stress and distress and provide the tools to prevent and manage future episodes:
1.	Distress Brief Intervention (DBI) Services Level 2
2.	Counselling and Therapeutic Support
3.	Group-Based Support and Wellbeing Activities
The outcomes the Council/RHSCP wish to see evidenced for people supported via this Service are as follows:
- Improvement in mental health and emotional wellbeing;
- Increased resilience, confidence, and self-management skills;
- Reduction in social isolation and loneliness;
- Strengthened social support networks and community connectedness;
- Enablement of individuals to achieve personal recovery goals and improve quality of life;
- Promotion of early intervention and prevention, reducing the need for more intensive statutory and specialist mental health services;
- Supporting people to maintain independence and participate fully in their communities.

North Northamptonshire Council is seeking to appoint a Provider to deliver Specialist Supported Housing Services to support the Council's Temporary Accommodation Pathway.
The service will provide and manage specialist supported accommodation for single homeless adults with high and multiple complex needs within North Northamptonshire.
The service forms part of the Council's wider homelessness prevention and temporary accommodation arrangements and will support individuals who require intensive housing management and person-centred support to achieve and sustain accommodation outcomes.
The service is intended to support individuals experiencing homelessness and rough sleeping, including those with complex and multiple support needs such as mental health challenges, substance misuse, offending histories, dual diagnosis and other barriers to independent living.
The successful Provider will be responsible for:
- Management and operation of the supported accommodation provision;
- Delivery of housing-related support and intensive housing management;
- Assessment, support planning and risk management;
- Safeguarding and welfare arrangements;
- Working collaboratively with health, substance misuse, criminal justice, housing and other support agencies;
- Supporting residents to progress towards independence and suitable move-on accommodation; and
- Monitoring and reporting service performance and outcomes.

London Tribunal Managed Service, (LTMS) which facilitate, amongst other things;
Data capture and case management for all appeals, statutory declarations and witness statements;
The timely and secure exchange of all appeal-related and non-appeal-related communications, notices, decisions, and correspondence between appellants, representatives, enforcement authorities, adjudicators, and London Tribunals staff, maintaining a complete and auditable record of all interactions;
Provide, maintain, and administer a publicly accessible statutory register, ensuring the timely publication, accuracy, integrity, retention, and accessibility of records in accordance with relevant statutory obligations and agreed service standards, and;
Deliver integrated hearing centre support services, including reception, administrative, clerical, security, and technical support, to facilitate the efficient delivery of remote, in-person, and hybrid hearings, and to ensure the effective operation of the Case Management System (CMS) and related digital services, etc.
The current services were procured under 4 distinct Lots:
Lot 1: Tribunal Services;
Lot 2: Tribunal Services, (RUCA), similar in scope to Lot 1 but with RUCA and GLA oversight;
Lot 3: Hardware ICT Infrastructure, necessary to operate the above services, and;
Lot 4: Trace, (Tow-Away, Removal and Clamping Enquiry) and TEC, (Traffic Enforcement Centre – Data transfer services), requiring the Lot 1 and 2 service provider to liaise with Trace and TEC which is an external resource.
The proposed procurement would be to retain Lot 1 and 2, but have the Hardware ICT Infrastructure and Trace and TEC as services managed by the successful supplier for Lot 1 and 2, as either a supply chain, (hardware) or managed service, (Trace and TEC).
London Councils estimate that there will be a maximum 1 year mobilisation of these services between the current incumbent and any new service provider, with the service contract being let by December 2027, to provide a one year transition and mobilisation between the current incumbent and any new provider.
London Councils would like to engage with the supply market to discuss these services and receive comment on the draft specification and procurement strategy, to allow feedback and impact both, before formally tendering the services. London Councils estimate that the UK 4 Tender Notice will be published early 2027, and has attached a procurement timeline indicating the process steps and dates.
Please note, this is a preliminary market engagement and presently not a formal public procurement, and London Councils does warrant of guarantee that there will be a formal procurement. All costs of engagement should be borne by the economic operators expressing interest in this opportunity.

The Council seeks to make provision for its electricity and gas requirements from 1st April 2027 for a period of three years with the option to extend for 2 x 12 months. The Council intends to procure a fully managed service whereby the Managed Service Provider (MSP) will purchase energy for and on behalf of the Council via an established framework or dynamic purchasing system that is compliant with either the previous or current
Procurement Regulations, which amounts to approximately £12,000,000 per annum within its Corporate, Unmetered Supply (UMS) and Schools portfolio including Academies. It will
provide a flexible purchasing method of procuring energy and will provide an end-to-end billing and management service. It will take the form of a single contract with the MSP negating the need to have a separate contract with the energy suppliers.
Deliveries of services included are as follows:
• Account Management including customer service;
• Billing;
• Management of Sites and Metering;
• Data Management;
• Purchase of green electricity;
• Complaints Handling;
• Online Management Portal;
• Portfolio Reviews;
• Performance Requirements and Service Levels
The contract provides for the possibility that the contract value may be modified by an increase or decrease during the contract term, in line with one or more of the following parameters: energy tariffs; energy volume consumption changes; applicable VAT rates; adjustments to school and academy participation; and/or upon exercise of the option to take up green gas supply. As that potential for modification to contract value has been specifically provided for in the contract, and as such a modification would not change the overall nature of the contract, it shall be considered by the contracting authority to be a permitted modification under section 74 and Schedule 8(1) of the Procurement Act 2023,
without the need for a new procurement.
The values given in this notice are only estimations and take into account the extension option - the Council gives no guarantee or warranty or makes any representation as to the accuracy of any indicative volumes.

