Renfrewshire Council has an existing Flexible Framework ("framework") for the provision of Care at Home and Housing Support Services under the direction of Renfrewshire Health and Social Care Partnership (RHSCP).
The framework allows RHSCP to purchase services to support (predominantly older) people to live independently in their own homes for as long as possible with the care and support they need being tailored to them, provided in a tailored and responsive manner. There may also be the occasional requirement for a Renfrewshire Council Children’s Services to arrange support services for a child.
The Service is delivered 24 hours per day, 52 weeks per year including public holidays.  The size of each individual package of care varies and is dependent on the individual’s assessed needs and outcomes.
As the services provided by this framework fall within the ‘Social and Other Specific Services’ category, and it is subject to the procurement regimes set out in section 7, Social and Other Specific Services, both of the Public Contracts (Scotland) Regulations 2015, a flexible approach was designed which allows “new entrants” to apply to be on the framework throughout the duration, as and when determined by RHSCP.
RHSCP now wish to reopen the framework to new entrants, to ensure capacity to deliver services across Renfrewshire. The process followed will be the same process used to appoint the original providers.  Any tenderer bidding for appointment to the framework will be required to satisfy the same minimum criteria as those awarded a place under the original tender process.
INTERESTED PARTIES MUST READ THE Invitation to Tender document FOR FULL DETAILS OF THE MINIMUM REQUIREMENTS FOR THIS FRAMEWORK.
- Technical and Professional Ability (2 examples of previous experience of delivering similar services. These MUST be within the last 3 years and demonstrate relevant experience and capacity);
- Care Inspectorate Requirements (Registered to deliver a Combined Support Service - Care at Home and a Housing Support Service (or ongoing application for Combined Service);
- Care Inspectorate minimum Quality Grade of 3 for all key questions;
- SSSC Registration and PVG Checks;
- Insurance Requirements;
- Financial Standing Requirements;
- Health and Safety Requirements;
- Cyber Security Requirements.
TENDERERS MUST MEET THE MINIMUM CRITERIA, OTHERWISE THEIR SUBMISSION WILL BE EXCLUDED FROM EVALUATION AND THEY WILL NOT BE APPOINTED TO THE FLEXIBLE FRAMEWORK.
Any Tenderer who does not meet the minimum requirements will not be eligible for appointment to the Flexible Framework but may participate in any future reopening of the Flexible Framework when these requirements have been met.

Background
Woodley Town Council is committed to supporting positive outcomes and opportunities for children and young people living in Woodley. Although the provision of youth services is not a statutory responsibility of the Town Council, the Council has a longstanding commitment to investing in services and activities that benefit local young people.
The Council's Strategy for Youth Services 2025-2030 provides the framework for how the Council will support youth provision during the strategy period. The Strategy has been developed following research and consultation with young people and relevant stakeholders and is intended to ensure that the Council's resources are used to support provision which is accessible, relevant and responsive to the needs of young people in Woodley.
The Council is now seeking quotations from suitably experienced organisations and individuals to deliver youth services and/or activities during the forthcoming financial year (1 April 2027 - 31 March 2028), in support of the aims and priorities set out in the Youth Strategy.
The opportunity
Woodley Town Council is inviting quotations from suitably experienced and appropriately qualified youth service providers, voluntary and community organisations, social enterprises and other organisations with relevant experience to propose the delivery of youth services and activities within Woodley.
The Council is deliberately adopting a flexible approach to the scope of this opportunity. It is not necessarily seeking a single provider to deliver all elements of youth provision. The Council may appoint one provider or a number of providers whose individual services and activities, taken together, contribute to the achievement of the Council's Youth Strategy objectives.
Proposals may therefore focus on a particular group of young people, a particular youth work approach, or a specific activity or programme.
For details of the current provision make up, please see the Council's Youth Service webpage. 
What the Council is looking for
The Council is particularly interested in proposals which:
engage children and young people broadly within the 9-21 age range;
provide positive, safe and inclusive opportunities for young people;
engage young people who may be vulnerable or at risk of becoming vulnerable;
reach young people who are not currently engaging with existing youth provision;
support young people's personal development, confidence, skills, wellbeing and connection with their community;
provide opportunities for young people to participate in activities which are interesting, relevant and accessible to them;
demonstrate an understanding of the local context and the needs of young people in Woodley; and
where appropriate, complement rather than duplicate existing provision and work collaboratively with relevant local organisations and partners.
The Council is particularly interested in provision taking place during school holidays and on Friday evenings, although proposals for other weekday evenings, weekends or particular dates/events will also be considered where they demonstrate a clear benefit to young people.
Types of provision
The Council is open to a broad range of proposals. Examples could include, but are not limited to:
regular activity-led youth sessions;
sports and physical activity;
arts, music, creative or cultural activities;
workshops, courses or training;
personal development, employability or life-skills activities;
mentoring or targeted support;
leadership and volunteering opportunities;
competitions and challenges;
structured trips or one-off activities;
targeted provision for particular groups or demographics; and
other innovative approaches which support the aims of the Youth Strategy.
Providers are encouraged to put forward proposals which they consider will have a meaningful and measurable benefit for young people rather than seeking to fit their service into a prescribed model.
Location and accessibility
The Council's priority is to fund provision which takes place within the Woodley Town Council boundary, or provision outside the boundary where the funding provides access and a clear benefit specifically to Woodley residents.
Providers should identify the proposed location(s) of their provision and explain how the proposed venue and delivery model will enable young people to access the service.
Outcomes and monitoring
Proposals should explain the outcomes the provider expects to achieve and how these will be measured.
The Council will be interested in evidence of both participation and impact. Depending on the nature of the service, this may include measures such as attendance, engagement with new participants, repeat participation, feedback from young people, progress against identified objectives, partnership working and other appropriate indicators.
Providers will be expected to provide appropriate monitoring information and reporting to the Council during the contract period.
Funding and contract period
The Council is due to consider the youth service budget for the forthcoming financial year in the Autumn, and will base this on the provision desired following responses to this invitation. For reference, the Council allocated £40k funding for youth provision for current financial year.
The Council reserves the right to appoint one provider or multiple providers and to determine the allocation of available funding between successful proposals.
Who should apply?
The opportunity is open to suitably experienced organisations and individuals with the skills, experience and appropriate safeguarding arrangements necessary to deliver services to children and young people.
The Council welcomes proposals from established youth service providers as well as voluntary and community organisations, social enterprises and other appropriately constituted organisations with relevant experience.
Providers should demonstrate:
relevant experience of working with children and/or young people;
appropriate safeguarding policies, procedures and practices;
appropriately qualified, experienced and vetted staff and/or volunteers;
suitable arrangements for managing risk;
appropriate insurance and governance arrangements; and
the capacity to deliver the proposed service reliably throughout the contract period.
Invitation to Quote
Interested providers are invited to submit a quotation and proposal addressing the requirements on the accompanying Supplier Response form.
Submissions should clearly set out:
the service or activities being proposed;
the young people the proposal is intended to engage;
the proposed location, frequency and timing of provision;
the intended outcomes and how these will be measured;
the experience and suitability of the proposed provider and delivery team;
safeguarding, risk management and other relevant operational arrangements;
proposed partnership or collaborative working arrangements, where applicable; and
the full cost of delivering the proposed service, including any relevant assumptions.
The Council is keen to encourage innovative and proportionate proposals which demonstrate a clear understanding of the Youth Strategy and provide meaningful opportunities for young people in Woodley.

