The HVM Catapult is a strategic research and innovation organisation for UK manufacturing. Established and supported by Innovate UK, it operates as a co-ordinated national network of six technology and innovation centres, working with industry, academia and government to accelerate industrial transformation and contribute to national missions.
The HVM Catapult centres are:
•	CPI; 
•	Manufacturing Technology Centre (MTC); 
•	National Manufacturing Institute Scotland (NMIS); 
•	NCC; 
•	University of Sheffield Advanced Manufacturing Research Centre (AMRC); and 
•	Warwick Manufacturing Group (WMG). 
Each centre provides distinct technological specialisms, sectoral focus and regional reach, while operating within a single Catapult framework. In addition to centre-level delivery, the Catapult undertakes network-wide functions including strategic leadership, cross-centre co-ordination, portfolio management, engagement with government and alignment with national missions.
HVM Catapult is looking to commission an independent evidence-based evaluation of the impact delivered through its core grant-funded activities with the report due by March 2027. 
HVM Catapult require an Early Findings Report, due by 18 December 2026, focusing on validating the evaluation framework, evidencing the rationale for intervention and establishing baseline measures for subsequent impact assessment. This early stage is intended to support strategic decision-making and will not be a substitute for the final evaluation. 
The final evaluation report should focus on four strategic priority areas and consider how they deliver workstreams, centre activities and therefore outputs, outcomes and KPIs. The four strategic priority areas to be considered are:
•	Industrial sustainability; 
•	Net zero: clean energy transition; 
•	Healthy living; and 
•	National security capability.
HVM Catapult may also, at its discretion, call off optional formative and learning evaluation services under the contract. These optional services are within the scope of the contract as awarded and will be provided at pre-agreed rates. HVM Catapult is under no obligation to call off any of the optional services.

The Sir John Brunner Foundation Multi-Academy Trust (Cheshire) is undertaking pre-market engagement in relation to a proposed procurement for the management of community lettings and facilities hire across part of its estate.
The initial scope is expected to include Middlewich High School and Sir John Deane's Sixth Form College, with the possibility of future extension to other Trust sites, subject to suitability, value for money and operational requirements. The Trust's wider estate includes The County High School, Leftwich and (from November) primary academies in the Northwich area. The Trust is seeking to understand the market for managed lettings services and to inform the development of its final specification, procurement route and commercial model. The proposed requirement is expected to involve the management, marketing, booking, administration and operational delivery of lettings and community use of school and college facilities outside core educational hours.
The Trust's objectives are to:
increase utilisation of facilities outside normal school and college hours;
improve and expand access for local clubs, voluntary organisations and community groups;
support sustainable community use, particularly for local and regular users;
ensure safeguarding, site security, supervision and operational control during lettings activity;
reduce the administrative and operational burden on academy staff;
protect the condition, readiness and availability of facilities for core educational use;
increase income through improved utilisation and broader use of facilities, rather than relying solely on price increases; and
establish a professional, compliant and scalable lettings model capable of operating across multiple academy sites where appropriate.
The facilities potentially within scope include, but are not limited to, indoor sports halls, outdoor pitches, all-weather/3G or astro facilities, playing fields, performance and assembly spaces, classrooms, specialist teaching areas, changing rooms, car parking and ancillary facilities. The final list of facilities will be confirmed following engagement with the market.
The Trust is particularly interested in engaging with providers able to deliver one or more of the following models:
a fully managed lettings service, including marketing, bookings, customer management, income collection, staffing, supervision, safeguarding, compliance and reporting;
a managed bookings and staffing model, including provider-led marketing, customer management and revenue administration, with operational delivery agreed between the provider and the Trust; and/or
advice on appropriate commercial structures, including revenue share, guaranteed income, management fee or hybrid models.
At this stage, the Trust does not intend to pursue a software-only booking platform as the primary solution, as the requirement includes a need for operational delivery, staffing and site supervision, particularly at Middlewich High School. However, the Trust welcomes feedback on systems and technology used to support managed lettings services.
The Trust anticipates that any future contract would require the successful provider to demonstrate robust arrangements for:
on-site staffing and supervision;
safeguarding, site security and incident escalation;
access control, opening, closing and site handover;
cleaning, facility reset and reporting of damage or defects;
customer service and complaints management;
protection and transition of existing community users where possible;
pricing strategy and affordability for local community users;
financial reporting, reconciliation, VAT information and income remittance;
performance reporting and attendance at formal contract review meetings; and
orderly exit and transfer of booking/customer data at the end of the arrangement.
The Trust is considering a phased implementation approach, with Sir John Deane's Sixth Form College and non-pitch lettings at Middlewich High School potentially commencing first, followed by Middlewich High School pitch or astro lettings at a later stage to allow appropriate transition and notice for existing users.
The estimated value of the initial opportunity is currently expected to be in the region of £420,000 to £480,000 or more over an initial three-year period, based on estimated gross lettings income across the sites. This estimate is indicative only and will be reviewed as the commercial model, scope and contract structure are developed. The final classification and value of any subsequent procurement will be confirmed before the formal tender stage.
The purpose of this pre-market engagement is to:
understand supplier capability and capacity in the managed school and college lettings market;
test the proposed scope, delivery model and phasing;
understand appropriate commercial models and risk allocation;
identify key operational, safeguarding, staffing and financial considerations;
inform the Trust's final specification and procurement documents; and
ensure that any subsequent procurement is proportionate, transparent and capable of delivering best value.
Participation in this pre-market engagement will not confer any advantage in any subsequent procurement process. Information shared by the Trust will be made available on a consistent basis where appropriate, and any future competition will be conducted in accordance with the Trust's procurement obligations, including the principles of transparency, equal treatment and non-discrimination. Interested providers are invited to respond to this notice and may be invited to participate in a site visit. Any future procurement opportunity will be advertised separately, and the Trust reserves the right not to proceed to procurement following this engagement.

The Living Well and Early Help offer is designed to be outcome focused looking at the diverse resources available within our community to meet our islands resident's needs. The current Living Well and Early Help Service on average each month supports over seven hundred people and provides over six hundred and fifty hours of support through their volunteers.This has been in place for 5 years (at expiry) and we need to continue for a further year alongside Public Health colleagues.
This will enable us to meet the overarching key objectives of:
• Increasing the ability of residents to participate in the planning of their care and support, and where they are able, to self-care to manage their own health conditions;
• Helping people to live well and gain, maintain or regain their independence to meet their own objectives;
• Reducing reliance on statutory services (GP's, acute hospital services and social care), delaying the need for emergency admission to hospital and/or permanent move into residential or nursing care.

