The Development Bank of Wales is seeking to appoint an appropriately qualified economic consultancy or research provider to undertake an independent review of the business finance landscape in Wales.
The Development Bank of Wales will act as the contracting authority and will enter into the contract with the successful supplier. Economic Intelligence Wales, the independent research arm of the Development Bank of Wales, is the project sponsor and commissioning body for the requirement. Economic Intelligence Wales will be responsible for shaping the research scope, providing subject matter input and supporting the delivery of the contract.
The review will assess how effectively current public, private and alternative finance provision meets the present and anticipated future needs of businesses in Wales. It will identify key gaps, frictions, affordability constraints, market failures, risks and opportunities within the Welsh business finance market.
The work is expected to provide the Development Bank of Wales, Welsh Government and wider stakeholders with an independent, robust and evidence-led assessment of the Welsh business finance landscape. The successful supplier will be required to produce practical, policy-relevant and decision-ready outputs suitable for senior policy, delivery and stakeholder audiences.
Core requirement
The initial commission will focus on Phase 1, which is expected to include:
mapping the current business finance landscape in Wales;
assessing the supply of, and demand for, business finance;
reviewing the role and availability of public, private and alternative finance;
identifying finance gaps, market frictions, affordability constraints and potential market failures;
examining the public finance and policy context;
assessing current and emerging trends affecting the future demand for and supply of finance;
identifying evidence and data gaps;
undertaking proportionate and targeted stakeholder engagement;
setting out headline policy and delivery implications; and
producing clear, credible and decision-ready findings and recommendations.
The detailed requirements, deliverables, milestones and reporting arrangements are set out in the procurement documents.
Optional services
Subject to the outcomes of Phase 1, budget availability, internal approvals and future commissioning decisions, the Development Bank of Wales may require additional thematic research or related services.
Potential additional work packages may include:
venture, innovation and scale-up finance;
ownership transition and succession finance;
commercial property, premises and development finance;
infrastructure, project finance and capital structuring;
additional data collection or stakeholder engagement;
focused updates or refreshes to the Phase 1 evidence base; and
final synthesis, policy analysis and reporting.
These additional work packages are not guaranteed. The Development Bank of Wales gives no commitment regarding the volume, value or timing of optional services that may be commissioned.

Westminster City Council is undertaking a comprehensive re-procurement programme for its housing repairs, maintenance and works contracts, currently delivered through a portfolio of Term Partnering Contracts (TPCs). The Programme forms part of the Council's wider strategic approach to the management, maintenance and improvement of its housing portfolio and is being delivered through a multi-tranche procurement programme supported by a Strategic Alliance model. The Council manages approximately 21,000 homes across the City, including a significant number of high-rise residential buildings, listed properties and buildings located within conservation areas. 
As part of Tranche 2 of the programme, the Council intends to procure a new borough-wide contract for Aids and Adaptations to replace the existing arrangement, which is due to expire in September 2027 subject to a 12-month extension being utilised.
The future contract is expected to form part of the Council's wider Strategic Alliance delivery model for housing repairs, maintenance and works services. The appointed provider may be required to enter into the Strategic Alliance arrangements and work collaboratively with other appointed providers to support the delivery of improved resident outcomes, innovation and value for money.
The existing Aids and Adaptations contract supports some of the Council's most vulnerable residents through the delivery of major and minor adaptations, planned works, responsive repairs, servicing, inspection and certification activities associated with specialist accessibility equipment and adaptations. The current contract is due to expire in September 2026, with a short-term extension proposed to facilitate the re-procurement process and ensure continuity of service.
The Council intends to undertake a competitive procurement exercise under the Procurement Act 2023 to appoint a single borough-wide provider to deliver the future service.  The future contract is currently anticipated to include, but not be limited to:
•	Delivery of major and minor adaptations following Occupational Therapist recommendations;
•	Installation, refurbishment and replacement of wet rooms and level access showers;
•	Installation, refurbishment and replacement of low-level and accessible kitchens;
•	Supply, installation and maintenance of stairlifts, ceiling track hoists, fixed lifting equipment and associated accessibility aids;
•	Responsive repairs, emergency repairs and out-of-hours support;
•	Planned preventative maintenance activities;
•	Inspection, servicing, maintenance and certification of specialist equipment;
•	Feasibility assessments and adaptation recommendations for occupied and void properties; and
•	Associated building works required to facilitate adaptations.
The estimated contract value stated in this Notice is indicative only and will be finalised prior to any Tender Notice being published.

